export const debate = {"number":"297","id":"winger-paulogia-resurrection-evidence-2018","date":"2026-10-07","title":"Mike Winger vs Paulogia — Evidence for the Resurrection","label":"Evidence for the Resurrection","topicCategory":"scripture-jesus-resurrection","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=myRsIqEOT1w","duration":"1 hr 16 min","motion":"Is there sufficient evidence to believe in the resurrection of Jesus?","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","sourceNote":"This assessment covers Mike Winger and Paulogia’s November 1, 2018 opening arguments, direct exchange and both closing statements preserved in this edited upload, approximately 3:24–1:19:34. The later retrospective introduction, on-screen corrections, moderator logistics and outro are excluded. The upload omits audience Q&A and some procedural interjections; none is imported into the assessment. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. Automatic captions were checked against source audio around one short gap; uncertain selected passages receive separate verification. The scores assess the arguments presented, not whether the resurrection occurred. All thirteen selected attribution checks are complete, using automated source-audio transcription and contextual comparison; no direct listening is claimed. Historical claims were not independently fact-checked. Featured quotations preserve exact source wording, while argument cards condense the exchange. The original recording linked by the publisher was compared to identify edits, without importing its omitted audience discussion.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance, rather than judgments about worldview truth; the separate AI Contribution does not affect these scores.","sides":{"pro":{"name":"Sufficient evidence supports the resurrection","speaker":"Mike Winger","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"The evidence is insufficient to establish the resurrection","speaker":"Paulogia","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":82,"con":84,"winner":"con"},"summary":"Mike Winger and Paulogia dispute whether early resurrection reports, appearances, persecution, and the empty tomb make Jesus’s resurrection the best historical explanation.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"jesus did in fact die by crucifixion","context":"Winger states the crucifixion as the shared historical starting point accepted for the debate, not as proof that a resurrection subsequently occurred."},"con":{"text":"history ultimately isn't a vote","context":"Paulogia acknowledges the relevance of specialist judgment while insisting that listeners still need the underlying arguments, sources, and sampling basis rather than a reported scholarly tally."}},"sections":[{"sectionId":"historical-method-and-sources","title":"Historical method and source standards","timebox":"5:02–22:05","score":{"pro":82,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-bible-as-historical-data","time":"5:02","score":82,"role":"Constructive","words":"Biblical texts can be assessed as historical documents without assuming inspiration, just as historians critically use the Quran or Hadith; Winger also appeals to distinct Gospel source streams.","critique":"Strongest feature: The admissibility distinction is clear and methodologically fair, preserving ordinary historical scrutiny without smuggling in scriptural authority or dismissing documents because communities later canonized them. Principal limitation: The reply asserts useful independence among sources more quickly than it demonstrates literary relationships, authorial access, dating, and the evidential effect of dependence. Live burden: Show which claims survive source criticism under competing dependence models, then explain why those independently supported claims materially favor resurrection over sincere transmission or legendary development. Locked score: 82 Its method is sound and directly useful, but the score remains below the top range because Winger names distinct sources without establishing their literary independence, provenance, or unequal evidential weight.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-probabilistic-history-standards","time":"17:59","score":86,"role":"Reply","words":"Historical judgment should remain probabilistic, and conventional explanations normally outrank extraordinary ones unless unusually strong evidence overcomes their lower prior probability and source uncertainty.","critique":"Strongest feature: The framework correctly makes historical conclusions graded rather than binary and places the comparative evidential burden on whoever advances the less antecedently expected explanation. Principal limitation: Its appeal to temporal distance and conventionality remains broad, because neither consideration is quantified or connected carefully to the reliability of each particular source claim. Live burden: Specify priors and likelihoods enough to compare resurrection with concrete mundane pathways, while avoiding an undefined appeal to extraordinary evidence that could immunize skepticism. Locked score: 86 The framework properly calibrates confidence and burden, though broad references to conventionality and distance are insufficient until applied to the particular sources and competing explanations in this case; the method becomes decisive only through claim-specific likelihood comparisons rather than a general appeal to extraordinariness.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-consensus-not-a-substitute","time":"20:26","score":87,"role":"Reply","words":"Scholarly agreement cannot replace accessible arguments and evidence, especially when the cited database, sampling rules, and underlying judgments are unavailable for inspection.","critique":"Strongest feature: The challenge properly separates expert testimony from a vote and asks listeners to evaluate the evidence that produced agreement rather than treating head counts as conclusions. Principal limitation: The database description, its sampling frame, and the claimed distribution are reported by a speaker rather than independently available here, which restricts how far the criticism can reach. Live burden: Identify representative arguments on each disputed fact and show whether disciplinary selection, definitions, or source judgments explain the apparent consensus instead of merely questioning its accessibility. Locked score: 87 The evidential principle is excellent and the database-access challenge is pertinent, but the criticism cannot establish sampling defects or disciplinary bias from speaker-reported information alone; its result is caution about the tally, not a contrary empirical finding about scholarly opinion.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"minimal-facts-and-grants","title":"Minimal facts and limited grants","timebox":"6:34–42:53","score":{"pro":85,"con":89},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-crucifixion-bedrock","time":"6:34","score":89,"role":"Constructive","words":"That Jesus died by crucifixion is a strong shared premise because both speakers accept it and treat it as historically secure background for evaluating later appearance claims.","critique":"Strongest feature: The move establishes a necessary chronological starting point with unusually careful calibration, relying on genuine common ground rather than exaggerating what the agreed crucifixion itself proves. Principal limitation: Death by crucifixion supplies no direct inference to a later resurrection, so its high security can lend borrowed confidence to a much more controversial conclusion. Live burden: Connect the death premise to independently supported postmortem evidence and demonstrate that the transition adds discriminatory force rather than simply preceding every proposed explanation. Locked score: 89 The premise earns very high marks for security and calibration, yet it remains a necessary background fact shared by resurrection and every mundane account rather than evidence that selects among them; its evidential value begins only when independently supported postmortem claims are connected to that death.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-limited-grants-on-witnesses","time":"31:21","score":88,"role":"Reply","words":"Paulogia grants that Paul believed he experienced Jesus and allows limited early appearance belief, while withholding claims about each disciple’s direct experience or the entire Twelve.","critique":"Strongest feature: The limited concessions are precise and prevent early belief, personal experience, group profession, and veridical appearance from collapsing into one stronger but unsupported proposition. Principal limitation: The position leaves uncertain exactly which named people and reports it accepts because the substantive person-specific attestation discussed remains sparse. Live burden: State a person-by-person evidential ledger for Peter, Paul, James, and the Twelve, distinguishing belief, profession, sensory experience, and independent corroboration. Locked score: 88 The careful scope of concession deserves high credit because it prevents a narrow historical core from silently expanding into direct attestation of all Twelve; the remaining limitation is that the cited evidence does not specify comparable direct attestation for every member represented by the collective title.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-paul-conversion-experience","time":"12:00","score":87,"role":"Constructive","words":"Paul’s sincere conviction that he encountered Jesus requires explanation, and his own letters provide early evidence that this belief transformed a former opponent of the movement.","critique":"Strongest feature: The argument appropriately distinguishes Paul’s belief and interpretation from the event’s ultimate reality while emphasizing a comparatively early, personally consequential claim. Principal limitation: It does not compare a visionary, neurological, psychological, or revelatory experience against a bodily encounter with enough detail to discriminate among them. Live burden: Explain which features of Paul’s own account are expected specifically under resurrection, and why rival experiences would predict those features substantially less well. Locked score: 87 Paul’s conviction is early, personal, and explanatorily important, although the debate does not identify features that distinguish a bodily encounter from another sincere revelatory or visionary experience; Paul’s reported transformation increases explanatory importance without independently settling the phenomenology that caused his conviction.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-creed-is-transmitted-report","time":"41:31","score":89,"role":"Reply","words":"The early creed shows that resurrection appearances were reported, but Paul’s transmission of received material does not make him a firsthand witness to the appearances it lists.","critique":"Strongest feature: The response sharply separates an early formula from direct testimony and therefore blocks the inference from early circulation to multiple independently verified encounters. Principal limitation: It gives insufficient attention to Paul’s later contacts with Peter and James, which could provide some opportunity to check portions of the inherited report. Live burden: Assess what Paul plausibly learned during those contacts, which clauses they could authenticate, and whether such checking changes the formula’s evidential independence. Locked score: 89 The report-witness distinction is decisive and precisely drawn, while Paul’s later contact with Peter and James leaves a limited possibility of checking that the reply does not fully assess; that contact question matters, but it cannot convert the entire list into separate testimony from every named group.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-twelve-attestation-reply","time":"39:22","score":81,"role":"Reply","words":"The creed and Gospel traditions support an early group-appearance claim, with “the Twelve” functioning as a recognized title rather than a literal headcount of twelve present witnesses.","critique":"Strongest feature: The reply usefully clarifies the titular language and points to an early group tradition instead of resting solely on later narrated appearance details. Principal limitation: A report concerning the Twelve is not twelve independent testimonies, and cross-Gospel consistency may reflect literary dependence or a common tradition rather than separate witnesses. Live burden: Establish the transmission chain and independence of the group report, then show why it records one shared encounter rather than a collective label applied retrospectively. Locked score: 81 The title clarification and focus on early group tradition answer a real narrowing move, but one transmitted collective report still cannot be counted as twelve independent testimonies; the remaining task is to establish one shared event rather than a recognized label for inherited group tradition.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"appearance-and-creed-evidence","title":"Appearance reports and the early creed","timebox":"7:35–56:18","score":{"pro":88,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-early-appearance-belief","time":"7:35","score":86,"role":"Constructive","words":"Very early resurrection belief and appearance traditions demand explanation because the creed locates them near the movement’s Jerusalem origins rather than centuries later.","critique":"Strongest feature: The argument correctly treats early belief as historically significant and uses the creed’s dating to constrain explanations that require a long period of legendary growth. Principal limitation: The independence, precise group scope, and experiential content of the reports remain contested, so early formulation alone cannot certify the events formulated. Live burden: Demonstrate which appearance propositions predate Paul, who endorsed them, and why their early spread is more probable after veridical encounters than after sincere reinterpretation. Locked score: 86 Early dating substantially constrains slow-development theories, but unresolved questions about the creed’s informants, dependence, and experiential content prevent the reported belief from functioning as verified appearance evidence; the unresolved issue is whether early formulation preserves multiple experiences or one rapidly authoritative interpretation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-gospel-source-limitations","time":"23:34","score":83,"role":"Reply","words":"The Gospels do not automatically provide independent eyewitness testimony because disputed authorship, literary dependence, theological purpose, and temporal distance affect how strongly their shared claims corroborate one another.","critique":"Strongest feature: The critique offers concrete testimonial criteria and correctly resists counting four canonical narratives as four independent witnesses merely because they appear in separate books. Principal limitation: Several claims about dependence, dating, anonymity, and theological construction are compressed or underdocumented within the debate, making the overall dismissal broader than its shown support. Live burden: Apply the criteria passage by passage, identifying genuinely independent material and explaining why each relevant tradition lacks the provenance needed for the proposed inference. Locked score: 83 The criteria are concrete and relevant, but several claims about authorship, dating, dependence, and theological construction receive too little defense to justify treating all narrative support alike; passage-level analysis is needed because dependence may weaken some attestations while leaving genuinely distinct material elsewhere.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-creed-historical-core","time":"42:51","score":90,"role":"Reply","words":"First Corinthians preserves an early historical core of reported appearances while leaving open whether those reports were true, mistaken, differently experienced, or dependent on one transmission stream.","critique":"Strongest feature: The move is highly responsive because it claims early testimony rather than automatically claiming truth, and it locates the formula near the people and place at issue. Principal limitation: Even a very early formula remains indirect for most listed people, and its compressed clauses do not reveal the experiences, informants, or verification behind them. Live burden: Trace each clause to identifiable sources where possible and test whether one transmission stream could account for the formula without multiple independent encounters. Locked score: 90 Its early date and explicit truth-report qualification make this an exceptionally disciplined reply, though unidentified informants and a compressed formula prevent independent verification of every listed encounter; Paul’s contacts may strengthen particular clauses, yet the exchange never establishes exactly what Peter or James verified.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-paul-vision-and-mundane-error","time":"29:30","score":77,"role":"Reply","words":"Paul’s visionary language allows a sincere mistaken experience to explain his resurrection belief, while deception remains a separate possibility for some professions rather than an explanation of sincere conviction.","critique":"Strongest feature: The response preserves the crucial distinction between a report and its cause, offering natural possibilities that remain compatible with Paul’s sincerity and later commitment. Principal limitation: The specific hallucination diagnosis is not established from Paul’s sparse descriptions, while loosely grouped alternatives do not yet form a comparative historical explanation. Live burden: Develop one evidence-sensitive account of Paul’s experience, specify its expected features, and compare those predictions with bodily resurrection rather than listing possibilities. Locked score: 77 The move successfully blocks an inference from sincerity to bodily resurrection, but Paul’s sparse descriptions leave the experience underdetermined among importantly different sincere possibilities; introducing deception addresses the origin of a profession, not the character of the sincere experience already granted.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-five-hundred-identifiability","time":"37:48","score":86,"role":"Reply","words":"The claim that five hundred people saw Jesus is difficult to test because the individuals are unnamed, potential denials would be practically undiscoverable, and the number comes through one transmitted formula.","critique":"Strongest feature: The city-and-concert analogy vividly exposes the identifiability problem and explains why a large number inside a report does not create hundreds of accessible attestations. Principal limitation: Practical unverifiability does not establish fabrication or mistake, and an ancient audience may have possessed social pathways unavailable to modern investigators. Live burden: Determine the formula’s source, intended audience access, and independent echoes, then judge whether the number adds evidence beyond the authority of the single transmitting tradition. Locked score: 86 The testability objection sharply limits the numerical claim’s force, although practical inaccessibility neither proves invention nor excludes ancient social routes by which some hearers might investigate the report; the numerical flourish therefore adds little beyond the credibility of the formula’s unidentified transmitting source.","tags":[]}},{"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-professed-versus-experienced","time":"55:42","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"What later Christians professed about group appearances must be distinguished from what named disciples individually experienced, believed, or could verify, because collective language can conceal different evidential relationships.","critique":"Strongest feature: The correction prevents communal repetition from being counted as direct perception and keeps the number of established experiencers from expanding through ambiguous collective language. Principal limitation: The debate supplies little positive evidence for how those professions arose, so the distinction limits an inference without explaining the tradition’s formation. Live burden: Identify which named individuals are directly evidenced as experiencers, believers, or later subjects of collective profession before drawing any group-level conclusion. Locked score: 82 The distinction prevents a major evidential inflation, yet it remains principally a blocking objection because the debate gives little evidence about how particular professions developed from earlier experiences or reports; no full alternative history is required merely to preserve this distinction and block the stronger evidential inference.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"bereavement-and-group-experience","title":"Bereavement experiences and group appearances","timebox":"44:09–47:14","score":{"pro":85,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-group-experience-not-hallucination","time":"45:59","score":85,"role":"Reply","words":"A genuinely shared perception would resist an individual-hallucination account because separate people simultaneously registering the same object ordinarily indicates an external cause rather than parallel private experiences.","critique":"Strongest feature: The argument isolates a real evidential discriminator: one coordinated group encounter would challenge explanations designed for private sensory episodes or isolated bereavement experiences. Principal limitation: It assumes the surviving group report accurately maps onto one simultaneous shared experience, which is precisely the contested historical proposition rather than an established observation. Live burden: First establish the event’s simultaneity, participants, and independence from later harmonization, then compare social suggestion and sequential interpretation with external appearance. Locked score: 85 A shared event would indeed challenge private hallucination, yet the historical record must first establish simultaneity, common content, and independent participants rather than presuppose them from collective wording; the argument’s force is conditional on proving the group datum rather than deriving it from the creed’s grammar.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-bereavement-experience","time":"44:09","score":83,"role":"Reply","words":"Common bereavement experiences show how sincere people might sense a deceased person’s presence without mental illness or an actual return to life.","critique":"Strongest feature: The proposal supplies a mundane mechanism compatible with sincere conviction and correctly presents it as one possibility rather than a complete reconstruction of every report. Principal limitation: General studies are not matched closely to the disciples’ circumstances, and typical brief auditory or presence experiences may differ from the debated group and bodily claims. Live burden: Identify which witness the mechanism explains, compare phenomenology and aftermath, and avoid extending an individual grief response to reports requiring separate social mechanisms. Locked score: 83 The mechanism establishes a credible route to sincere error for a bereaved individual, but the reported general studies are not matched closely to these witnesses or to bodily and group claims; its reach is a possible origin for some individual conviction, not every appearance report.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"explanatory-burden-and-alternatives","title":"Explanatory burden and natural alternatives","timebox":"12:47–1:19:38","score":{"pro":75,"con":78},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-resurrection-best-explanation","time":"12:47","score":76,"role":"Constructive","words":"Resurrection is initially offered as a unified explanation of crucifixion, early appearance belief, Paul’s conversion, and group reports, thereby competing with rival accounts on total explanatory fit.","critique":"Strongest feature: The inference seeks explanatory unity and addresses several data points together, which is stronger than treating each tradition as an unrelated curiosity. Principal limitation: The comparison remains incomplete because priors, source dependence, expected evidence, and rival mechanisms are not analyzed consistently across the same evidential set. Live burden: Define the competing hypotheses and their likelihoods, then show why resurrection predicts the qualified evidence better enough to overcome its much lower conventional prior. Locked score: 76 The unifying ambition is valuable, but the comparison does not specify priors, source dependence, or likelihoods for coherent rivals, leaving the claimed explanatory victory more asserted than demonstrated; a persuasive comparison would test one evidence set under resurrection, sincere error, deception, and social transmission.","tags":[{"type":"bias","label":"Base-rate neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/","context":"Resurrection's local fit to selected reports is emphasized without consistently weighing its background probability against specified mundane alternatives."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-miracle-burden","time":"22:04","score":84,"role":"Reply","words":"Miracles may be possible in principle while still requiring unusually strong evidence because a resurrection begins with a much lower historical prior than ordinary error, transmission, or misperception.","critique":"Strongest feature: The argument carefully distinguishes impossibility from improbability and locates disagreement in comparative evidential burden rather than simply banning supernatural events. Principal limitation: Calling a miracle least probable by definition risks circularity unless base rates and theological background assumptions are stated, especially when the hypothesis itself challenges ordinary regularities. Live burden: Explain how priors are assigned without presupposing naturalism and what observable evidence could realistically raise resurrection above specified mundane alternatives. Locked score: 84 The possibility-probability distinction is strong, yet describing miracle as least probable risks circularity unless background assumptions and possible likelihood-ratios are stated without presupposing the disputed worldview; the standard must also identify what evidence could overcome the prior, preserving the explicit concession of possibility.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-selective-appearances-fit","time":"49:25","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"Selective appearances followed by cessation fit the texts better than contagious mass experience, because later hearers are not portrayed as immediately seeing Jesus and Paul calls his appearance last.","critique":"Strongest feature: The response draws a specific predicted-pattern contrast and notes that Paul’s “last of all” language conflicts with indefinitely spreading visionary contagion. Principal limitation: It relies on the disputed textual pattern as accurate history and overstates scholarly consensus while leaving socially transmitted reinterpretation distinct from simultaneous hallucination. Live burden: Show that the appearance sequence is independently attested and explain why memory convergence or selective testimony could not produce the same bounded pattern. Locked score: 82 The bounded-pattern objection targets the proposed mechanism directly, while its force depends on accepting the transmitted sequence as accurate history and not as later selective organization; the reply also must distinguish contagious experience from later convergence in how already bounded reports were remembered.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Appeal to authority","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/","context":"Winger says the bare-bones case forces Paulogia to deny scholarly consensus on multiple eyewitness testimony."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-social-transmission-alternative","time":"47:12","score":71,"role":"Reply","words":"A few initial experiences or deceptions could spread through memory, suggestion, and communal reinforcement until others sincerely professed a broader appearance tradition without sharing one simultaneous hallucination.","critique":"Strongest feature: The move identifies a social mechanism distinct from shared hallucination, so it can explain expanding profession without claiming many people experienced one identical private perception. Principal limitation: Its anecdotes are loose, sometimes imprecisely described, and never integrated into a chronological account connecting Peter, the Jerusalem community, Paul, and the creed. Live burden: Build a source-constrained sequence with dates, agents, and transmission steps, then show that each step predicts the particular early claims rather than generic rumor. Locked score: 71 Distinguishing social spread from shared hallucination is conceptually important, but the anecdotes and memory claims never become a source-constrained sequence connecting early individuals, communities, Paul, and the creed; until those links are supplied, the mechanism remains possible and relevant but not a demonstrated sufficient history.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-uncommitted-alternatives-challenge","time":"52:08","score":74,"role":"Reply","words":"A list of incompatible natural possibilities does not by itself outperform resurrection, because comparative explanation requires assessing each coherent rival and relevant combination against the same qualified evidence.","critique":"Strongest feature: The challenge correctly observes that bare possibilities cannot simply be pooled as though each independently weak suggestion formed one mutually supporting historical explanation. Principal limitation: It unfairly raises the negative side’s burden by implying skepticism must reconstruct a complete history, and it asserts incompatibility without testing distinct alternatives separately. Live burden: Compare resurrection against the strongest single mundane hypothesis and relevant mixtures, while allowing underdetermination when neither side earns a sufficiently high posterior probability. Locked score: 74 Bare possibilities do not automatically cumulate into a superior explanation, but skepticism need not supply one complete rival history when the affirmative has not established a sufficiently discriminating likelihood comparison; underdetermination remains available when resurrection and its rivals all depend on material assumptions not independently established.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/","context":"Paulogia is required to supply one coherent full reconstruction while resurrection is defended through broad comparative fit without equivalent detail."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-closing-insufficiency","time":"1:17:19","score":79,"role":"Reply","words":"The evidence does not require resurrection if a small number of sincere errors or deceptions can generate the limited facts actually established, though that proposed mechanism still needs adequate fit.","critique":"Strongest feature: The conclusion properly returns to sufficiency and reminds listeners that an explanation’s compatibility with evidence does not make it necessary or most probable. Principal limitation: Its two-person model remains underdeveloped and does not adequately derive group reports, the creed’s particular list, or the contested empty-tomb tradition. Live burden: Present a causal sequence that generates each accepted fact and compare its independent assumptions, expected traces, and residual anomalies with the resurrection hypothesis. Locked score: 79 Returning to sufficiency properly preserves the negative burden, but the asserted two-person pathway remains too thin to derive the group formula and contested tomb evidence it claims to explain; because the closing affirmatively claims adequacy, it bears a limited burden to connect that mechanism to each accepted fact.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-closing-worldview-and-best-explanation","time":"1:18:22","score":67,"role":"Reply","words":"Winger concludes that resurrection best unifies the accepted facts and argues that an extremely low prior prevents the evidence from supporting it, but Paulogia explicitly allows miracles in principle.","critique":"Strongest feature: The closing states a direct comparative conclusion and correctly presses the question of whether background worldview commitments influence historical probability judgments. Principal limitation: It severely caricatures a low prior as impossibility and treats demands for stronger evidence as indifference to evidence, bypassing Paulogia’s explicit concession of possibility. Live burden: Recast the dispute quantitatively or comparatively, acknowledge reasonable low priors, and show how the evidence overcomes them without attributing bad faith or closed-mindedness. Locked score: 67 The conclusion is direct, but its lowest score reflects the serious caricature of low prior probability as impossibility or evidential indifference despite Paulogia’s explicit contrary qualification; a stronger closing would grant reasonable low priors and argue conditionally that the specified evidence nevertheless overcomes them.","tags":[{"type":"fallacy","label":"Red herring","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/","context":"Paulogia's low-prior position is recast as impossibility and indifference to evidence instead of answering whether the evidence overcomes that prior."}]}}]},{"sectionId":"sincerity-persecution-and-recantation","title":"Sincerity, persecution, and recantation","timebox":"56:15–1:07:45","score":{"pro":80,"con":92},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-acts-and-early-suffering-sources","time":"56:54","score":78,"role":"Reply","words":"Acts, First Clement, Polycarp, and related traditions are used to argue that named early Christian leaders persisted through persecution because they sincerely believed their resurrection proclamation.","critique":"Strongest feature: The move cites multiple sources and makes the relevant modest point that costly persistence bears on sincerity more directly than on the event’s truth. Principal limitation: It shifts between Peter, Paul, Stephen, the Twelve, apostles generally, and later Christians, while the evidence does not support one universal recantation opportunity. Live burden: Separate each named person, source, date, and claimed suffering, granting ordinary sincerity evidence from costly persistence while reserving personal knowledge and recantation conditions for the narrower inference against conscious fabrication. Locked score: 78 Multiple early sources provide some sincerity evidence, but shifting among Peter, Paul, Stephen, the Twelve, and apostles generally prevents the argument from supporting one universal recantation opportunity; person-specific mapping is required before persistence can corroborate firsthand knowledge or a knowing refusal to retract.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-first-witness-persecution-gap","time":"56:15","score":91,"role":"Reply","words":"Persecution supports eyewitness sincerity only when a named person could assess the resurrection claim, suffered for maintaining that claim, and could avoid punishment by specifically recanting it.","critique":"Strongest feature: The objection identifies the exact missing links between suffering, personal epistemic access, and a genuine recantation option, preventing generic persecution from proving witness truthfulness. Principal limitation: The diagnostic question alone does not disprove particular cases, especially Peter or Paul, for whom persistence and proximity may still carry limited evidential weight. Live burden: Evaluate each proposed martyr separately and specify the persecutor’s demand, the witness’s knowledge, the available escape, and the source supporting every link. Locked score: 91 The move identifies every missing link with exceptional accuracy, though asking the diagnostic question does not erase limited sincerity evidence for named figures where proximity and persistence are better supported; its calibrated conclusion limits group-wide corroboration rather than demanding that the negative prove nobody faced such conditions.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-recantation-inference","time":"1:04:28","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"Repeated threats and continued preaching make it reasonable to infer that prominent leaders knew silence or recantation could reduce danger, thereby supporting their sincerity rather than the event’s truth.","critique":"Strongest feature: The response candidly labels the conclusion inferential and draws cumulative support from imprisonment, threats, return to hostile settings, and early testimony about steadfastness. Principal limitation: Its evidence is strongest for named leaders such as Peter and Paul and cannot justify claims about every disciple or a coordinated full-group refusal. Live burden: Limit the conclusion person by person and distinguish continued preaching from an explicit chance to deny a personally witnessed bodily appearance. Locked score: 82 Its candor about inferential status and cumulative threats makes the sincerity case respectable, although evidence for named leaders cannot sustain a universal conclusion about every alleged appearance witness; continued preaching shows durable commitment most clearly, while its connection to personally witnessed bodily appearances remains uneven.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-person-specific-recantation-gap","time":"1:04:04","score":93,"role":"Reply","words":"No broad martyrdom argument succeeds without evidence that each alleged witness faced punishment for the relevant resurrection claim and possessed a genuine opportunity to escape by recanting it.","critique":"Strongest feature: The distinction is exceptionally precise, separating death, persecution, Christian identity, resurrection preaching, firsthand knowledge, and an actionable offer to recant. Principal limitation: It remains a demanding evidential challenge rather than positive proof of insincerity, and it does not erase the limited significance of Peter’s or Paul’s persistence. Live burden: Apply the test consistently to every named individual, preserving modest sincerity evidence where links exist and withholding it where later collective traditions supply only labels. Locked score: 93 The distinction among death, identity, firsthand knowledge, relevant accusation, and escape by recantation is exceptionally rigorous, while remaining compatible with modest persistence evidence for Peter and Paul; the objection restricts scope and does not undertake the stronger claim that no witness ever had such an opportunity.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"empty-tomb-and-mark","title":"Empty tomb, embarrassment, and Mark","timebox":"13:46–1:17:11","score":{"pro":81,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-empty-tomb-corroboration","time":"13:46","score":77,"role":"Constructive","words":"The empty tomb gains support from women discoverers, Jerusalem proclamation, hostile-response traditions, burial details, and its acceptance by a scholarly majority.","critique":"Strongest feature: The case assembles several potentially convergent considerations and carefully distinguishes a majority judgment from the stronger consensus claimed for the minimal facts. Principal limitation: The considerations are compressed and not independently verified here, while arguments from silence, hostile explanations, and disciplinary majorities are pressed beyond their demonstrated reach. Live burden: Analyze provenance and dependence for each consideration, then test whether burial uncertainty, literary design, or later tradition predicts the same surviving record. Locked score: 77 The accumulation of considerations gives the tomb real argumentative breadth, while their compressed presentation, contested independence, and reliance on reported scholarship keep the cumulative case from becoming secure; especially vulnerable are arguments from hostile response, silence, and majority status when their underlying support remains compressed.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-empty-tomb-status-challenge","time":"1:09:06","score":75,"role":"Reply","words":"Paulogia reports that Habermas omitted the empty tomb and questions whether its majority rests mainly on New Testament specialists, while Winger distinguishes this majority claim from his three consensus facts.","critique":"Strongest feature: The objection correctly distinguishes majority from consensus and asks whether disciplinary selection changes the apparent level of agreement about the tomb. Principal limitation: The conference report and explanation of Habermas’s choice are not independently verified, and scholarly status alone does not answer the underlying historical arguments. Live burden: Present the relevant survey definitions and samples, then evaluate the tomb directly rather than treating a reported change in one scholar’s presentation as decisive. Locked score: 75 The consensus-majority distinction is correctly preserved, but Paulogia’s conference report and disciplinary explanation remain unverified, and scholarly status cannot substitute for evaluating the tomb evidence itself; Winger had already labeled the tomb majority-supported rather than one of his consensus facts, narrowing the objection’s target.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-criterion-and-mark-theme-reply","time":"1:11:44","score":82,"role":"Reply","words":"Embarrassing or authorially inconvenient details can support historicity, and Mark’s private explanations to disciples and Peter’s insight complicate the claim that outsiders understand while insiders consistently fail.","critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly states the criterion’s logic and supplies textual counterexamples to the proposed Markan theme instead of relying only on scholarly authority. Principal limitation: It does not settle the women’s narrative function, and it too quickly calls the thematic alternative ad hoc without tracing Mark’s broader characterization. Live burden: Show why the women-at-tomb detail conflicts with Mark’s literary interests and compare that cost with ordinary burial custom and thematic usefulness. Locked score: 82 Textual counterexamples and a clear criterion explanation make the response effective, but they do not settle the women’s literary function or show that customary-role explanations are merely ad hoc; Peter’s insight and private explanations complicate, but do not eliminate, Mark’s recurrent disciple failures within a comprehensive narrative analysis.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-embarrassment-correction-and-limit","time":"1:10:59","score":81,"role":"Reply","words":"Paulogia corrects “only theologians” to New Testament studies and argues that women at the tomb fit burial custom and Mark’s literary reversal, reducing their force as embarrassing testimony.","critique":"Strongest feature: The correction is explicit, while the customary and literary alternatives show why an apparently awkward detail need not be included against authorial interest. Principal limitation: The disciplinary-scope claim is unsupported within the exchange, and the broad theme is asserted more readily than demonstrated across Mark’s varied disciple portrayals. Live burden: Defend the claimed concentration of the criterion within New Testament studies and substantiate the Markan theme, while answering Winger’s contrary example rather than assuming its irrelevance. Locked score: 81 The explicit correction and two plausible alternatives significantly reduce a simplistic criterion, while the claim about disciplinary confinement and the broad Markan theme receive limited support in the exchange; its strongest result is interpretive underdetermination, not proof that Mark invented the women or the empty tomb.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-mark-ending-context","time":"1:16:07","score":87,"role":"Reply","words":"Mark’s messenger commands the women to tell Peter and the disciples, while a parallel command to say nothing can indicate silence during an instructed journey rather than permanent nondisclosure.","critique":"Strongest feature: The close reading is strongly responsive because it uses immediate context and an internal linguistic parallel to challenge an interpretation based solely on the final verse. Principal limitation: The proposed danger rationale is not established by the text, and compatibility with eventual reporting does not itself prove the tomb narrative historical. Live burden: Defend the semantic parallel in Greek and narrative context, then separate what Mark implies happened afterward from whether that implication preserves an actual event. Locked score: 87 The command and internal parallel strongly rebut permanent silence, though the proposed danger rationale lacks textual support and narrative compatibility alone cannot establish the tomb’s historicity; the semantic parallel addresses disclosure within the story but leaves source provenance and event truth as separate questions.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-mark-theme-and-ending-objection","time":"1:15:40","score":81,"role":"Reply","words":"Mark’s abrupt ending leaves the disciples uninformed within the narrated text, fitting a pattern in which outsiders and women perceive what insiders repeatedly miss and weakening an embarrassment inference.","critique":"Strongest feature: The objection gives the ending coherent literary function and resists importing later appearances merely because the messenger predicts a meeting in Galilee. Principal limitation: The wider theme is defended only briefly, and a meaningful ending does not show that the women, tomb, or command were invented rather than interpreted. Live burden: Trace the motif across Mark while explaining why the explicit command and predicted appearance do not intentionally point beyond the written ending to known tradition. Locked score: 81 The abrupt ending receives a coherent literary interpretation, yet the briefly defended theme and narrated silence do not establish that the tomb, women, or command were invented; the messenger’s explicit command and promised Galilee meeting still require interpretation within that literary account without presupposing permanent nondisclosure.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":82,"strengths":["Separates admitting biblical documents as historical sources from assuming their inspiration or inerrancy.","Uses the early creed with careful qualification as evidence of early appearance testimony rather than automatic proof.","Directly answers the Mark ending objection through the messenger’s command and a relevant internal parallel.","Acknowledges that martyrdom evidence is inferential and strongest for particular named leaders."],"blunders":[{"text":"Caricatures a low prior for resurrection as assuming impossibility or refusing any historical evidence.","links":[]},{"text":"Moves from collective labels and general persecution to claims stronger than the person-specific sources support.","links":[]},{"text":"Treats an incompletely specified set of alternatives as affirmative confirmation of resurrection.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":84,"strengths":["Consistently distinguishes early reports, sincere professions, personal experiences, and events that actually occurred.","Exposes the missing person-specific links between persecution, eyewitness knowledge, recantation opportunity, and continued suffering.","Allows miracles in principle while demanding comparative evidence proportionate to their low ordinary probability.","Separates social transmission from simultaneous group hallucination and thereby avoids conflating different mechanisms."],"blunders":[{"text":"Offers illustrative social mechanisms without showing how they fit the particular early reports and transmission sequence.","links":[]},{"text":"Reports disciplinary and conference claims about consensus without evidence available for independent verification.","links":[]},{"text":"Uses a thin two-person closing model that does not derive every appearance and tomb tradition discussed.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The resurrection becomes the strongest explanation when early testimony, costly conviction, group claims, and the tomb are assessed together under an open supernatural prior.","premises":["Jesus’s accepted crucifixion fixes a genuine death that any adequate explanation must connect to the rapid rise of resurrection proclamation.","Paul’s letters preserve early appearance traditions and his own transformative conviction close enough to the movement’s origins to constrain slow legendary accounts.","If a coordinated group encounter is historically established, private hallucination alone cannot explain why several people attributed one shared event to Jesus.","The debated empty-tomb considerations add conditional corroboration, although their force depends on source independence, burial confidence, and literary analysis.","A hypothesis gains comparative force when it explains otherwise diverse evidence with fewer independent auxiliary assumptions than its strongest rival."],"conclusion":"If the creed and group reports are sufficiently early and independent, Paul’s experience is better expected on resurrection, and the tomb evidence survives source criticism, resurrection is conditionally the best explanation of the qualified record."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Explanatory Coupling","text":"Suppose the creed, Paul’s conversion, and the bounded appearance sequence are sufficiently independent to provide partly separate evidence about an early cause or causes. Their temporal concentration and common reference to Jesus can then be compared under a single resurrection explanation and under multiple psychological or social causes. Resurrection gains force only if it predicts that pattern more economically and accurately; independence permits this comparison but does not itself require one cause."},{"title":"Costly Correction Test","text":"If named leaders repeatedly encountered informed opponents while preaching in Jerusalem, persistent proclamation could become evidence beyond mere sincerity: public contradiction should have supplied opportunities to correct easily exposed factual claims. This line requires proof about what opponents knew, what was publicly testable, and which exact claims leaders maintained; otherwise social commitment can explain persistence without confirming resurrection."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Historical restraint is warranted because the secure evidence primarily establishes early sincere belief and transmitted reports, not independently verified bodily encounters or an empty tomb.","premises":["An early creed dates a claim but does not transform its compressed list into multiple accessible and independent eyewitness depositions.","Paul’s sincerity can be granted while the nature and cause of his experience remain underdetermined by his limited descriptions.","Collective profession can emerge through interpretation, memory convergence, and social reinforcement without a simultaneous shared hallucination or deliberate conspiracy.","Costly persistence can support ordinary sincerity, while the narrower inference against conscious fabrication requires a named knower punished for the relevant assertion with a meaningful opportunity to recant.","A low-frequency event requires evidence whose likelihood ratio over ordinary error is strong enough to compensate for its lower prior."],"conclusion":"Because the exchange leaves source independence, group phenomenology, martyr-specific recantation, and the tomb contested, withholding resurrection as the best historical conclusion remains rational without declaring miracles impossible."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Compression Asymmetry","text":"Creedal compression can preserve a community’s stable conclusion while erasing differences among the experiences that produced it. Diverse dreams, visions, inherited reports, and interpretive memories may therefore appear later as one uniform list of appearances. This predicts formulaic agreement without requiring either coordinated deception or identical hallucination, and it shifts inquiry toward the pre-formula transmission process that the debate leaves largely unobserved."},{"title":"Correlated Evidence","text":"When several major data points pass through a small, partly connected source network, they cannot be multiplied as though each were fully independent. Their combined force depends on conditional relationships: one report may still add information about another, and evidence that a source is reliable can raise confidence across dependent claims. The historical task is therefore to model dependence and reliability explicitly, then ask how strongly the resulting network discriminates resurrection from ordinary transmission."}]}},"year":2018,"assessmentScopeDisclosure":"This assessment covers Mike Winger and Paulogia’s November 1, 2018 opening arguments, direct exchange and both closing statements preserved in this edited upload, approximately 3:24–1:19:34. The later retrospective introduction, on-screen corrections, moderator logistics and outro are excluded. The upload omits audience Q&A and some procedural interjections; none is imported into the assessment. Neither speaker receives credit, penalties or an inference of failing to reply from excluded material. Automatic captions were checked against source audio around one short gap; uncertain selected passages receive separate verification. The scores assess the arguments presented, not whether the resurrection occurred."};
